WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:03.830 align:start position:0%
 
every moment  is filled with worry for emma mother to  a teenager  who's among thousands of children  waiting for specialist treatment for an  eating disorder emma's daughter who we  won't name in order to protect her  identity  is in a children's hospital but she's  not getting the complete care she needs  at the moment she's not with people that  are trained  to deal with mental health and she's  stuck in a room all the time she's not  getting to engage in activities with  other children she doesn't really do  much she's not allowed out of the  building  i just think she's getting worse in  there  nhs data on eating disorders shows a  four-fold increase in the number  of children and young people waiting for  urgent care  if you're not getting that special input  on people who understand how the eating  disorder can  really start to affect someone's mental  health then  generally it's likely that it could  deteriorate they're not getting the  right treatment so they're not getting  the people that can support them to  really start to fight that voice in  someone's head  nhs england says it's investing in  mental health through things like  increased funding and the introduction  of helplines  a spokesperson told us thanks to the nhs  long-term plan  an additional 345 000 children and young  people  each year will get help with their  mental health and well-being  and 40 million pounds has also been  invested in additional inpatient beds  so if you are struggling or have a child  who needs help  please continue to come forward to get  the care you or they  need so that's her in the hospital  emma says this hasn't worked now in her  fourth month of waiting  her daughter she says makes two attempts  every day  to run away from hospital last week she  was found  three miles away she just wanted to get  away  she got really scared she was sat in a  field for like an hour  and um this was after she were crying  and hugging me and she was saying mom i  were really scared i never thought i'd  see you ever again  i was sat in this field and it was pitch  black the hospital told us its clinical  teams are trying to provide the best  care possible  and that it's working with the nhs to  help secure a suitable place for emma's  daughter's care  but she says the longer her child has to  wait  the more her condition worsens sadia  chowdhury sky news  in sheffield

